<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Faiyum</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fayum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Faiyum is a large depression and oasis west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, watered by a branch of the river, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahr-yusuf">Bahr Yusuf</a>, and containing Lake Qarun. Twelfth Dynasty kings, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>, reclaimed land there, and the Ptolemies settled Greek veterans in it. It is famous for its Roman-period mummy portraits and papyri.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/faiyum.jpg" alt="Faiyum" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roland Unger · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MadinatFayyumWaterWheel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Amenemhat I founded a new royal residence, Amenemhat-itj-tawy ('Amenemhat seizes the Two Lands'), usually shortened to Itjtawy, near el-Lisht at the entrance to the <b>Faiyum</b>, moving the centre of government back towards the old Memphite heartland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Tens of thousands of papyri, above all from Oxyrhynchus, the <b>Faiyum</b> villages and Karanis, record leases, taxes, lawsuits, letters and school exercises.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Egyptian left traces in the Arabic spoken in Egypt, in place names such as Aswan and <b>Faiyum</b>, and in the vocabulary of other languages: ebony, oasis, gum and adobe come from Egyptian words.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>Many were settled in the <b>Faiyum</b>, which the Ptolemies drained and colonised with Greeks and Egyptians, founding villages such as Philadelphia and Karanis.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 2160 BCE a family from Herakleopolis Magna, near the entrance to the <b>Faiyum</b>, took over the kingship from the Eighth Dynasty of Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/state/herakleopolitan-kingdom">Herakleopolitan Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
